The house rule is a clean, organized playground for real work, at any project size. Anything useful can go in if it passes the cartridge test. Label it, push it in, pull it out, keep it self-contained, and make it easy to throw away.

Everything built here must do real work. `VISION.md` rejects toy examples that exist only to demonstrate a pattern.

rat-stack is a reference that other projects vendor like a library.

Its example CLI inspects real files. Its [database cartridge](/systems/database) owns a `RunLog` service and two vendor Layers. The website's search and read [capabilities](/systems/capabilities) serve the public repo. Each piece does real work.

The clean-playground test asks what remains when one bin is removed. Removal must leave other packages' import paths unchanged, keep bindings contained, and pass the full gate.

`VISION.md` describes the cartridge test. `AGENTS.md` assigns each package one job and sets the gate.

[Analytics](/systems/analytics) supplies another working bin, with an explicit removal checklist. [Interest signup](/systems/interest) keeps delivery in an [adapter package](/lore/hexagonal-architecture) outside core. One-line infrastructure removal remains unproven for that adapter.

See [cartridges](/lore/cartridges) for the infrastructure example and [the fence](/lore/the-fence) for the checks that make removal honest.
